  "12915363": {
    "cueTypes": [
      "bias-awareness"
    ],
    "verification": "abstract-read",
    "safeflowSummary": "Croskerry's foundational 2003 paper catalogues the specific cognitive biases - which he termed \"cognitive dispositions to respond\" - that drive diagnostic error, and argues these are addressable through deliberate \"cognitive debiasing\" strategies rather than accepted as an unavoidable cost of fast clinical judgement. The strategy given the most weight is metacognition: consciously stepping back from the immediate problem to examine one's own reasoning process before committing to a decision. SafeFlow's bias-awareness cue draws on this framework - naming the specific bias at play (anchoring, premature closure, and so on) is what makes the reflective step-back concrete rather than a vague exhortation to \"think again\"."
  },
