  "26294709": {
    "cueTypes": [
      "safety-ii"
    ],
    "verification": "abstract-read",
    "safeflowSummary": "Braithwaite, Wears and Hollnagel's 2015 paper is the source of the Safety-I/Safety-II distinction SafeFlow's \"what went well\" strand is built on: where Safety-I looks backwards to find and fix what went wrong, Safety-II asks what let performance go right on the vast majority of shifts where nothing did. The authors' central claim is that healthcare is resilient largely because clinicians are constantly, invisibly adjusting what they do to match shifting conditions - and that naming and reinforcing those adjustments, not just auditing failures, is what improves safety going forward. SafeFlow's debrief prompts for \"what went well\" are a direct, ward-level application of this: surfacing the adjustments a nurse made that kept a shift safe, not only the moments that nearly went wrong."
  },
