  "34888450": {
    "cueTypes": [
      "family-concern"
    ],
    "verification": "abstract-read",
    "safeflowSummary": "Bucknall and colleagues' 2021 paper reviews family-activated rapid response systems - mechanisms that let a patient or relative alert a hospital's critical care outreach team directly, bypassing the ward's usual escalation chain, when their concerns about deterioration are not being acted on by the primary team. The UK's own version, the \"Call for Concern\" model championed by the Royal Berkshire Hospital, predates Martha's Rule and is one of the precedents the newer national policy draws on; the authors note implementation is genuinely difficult and depends on both staff and family understanding when and how to use it. SafeFlow's family-concern scenario is grounded in this literature: family escalation is framed as a distinct, legitimate safety channel, not merely a variant of staff intuition."
  },
