  "38832236": {
    "cueTypes": [
      "new-to-service"
    ],
    "verification": "abstract-read",
    "safeflowSummary": "A 2024 systematic review of 17 studies (16 qualitative) found that internationally educated nurses' integration into host health systems is too often treated as a one-way process - new starters expected to adapt to the ward, rather than the ward adapting to them - and that a mismatch between pre-migration expectations and on-the-ground reality is a recurring theme across settings. The review's authors call for a comprehensive, collaborative organisational response that addresses discrimination, racism, and communication or language barriers directly, since these - not just clinical unfamiliarity - are what most affects job satisfaction and retention. For SafeFlow, this supports treating a \"new to this ward\" flag as a prompt for the team to adapt its own practice, not just a note that the new starter needs extra supervision."
  },
  "39026330": {
    "cueTypes": [
      "documentation"
    ],
    "verification": "abstract-read",
    "safeflowSummary": "In a prospective controlled trial across a Chinese tertiary ICU, introducing an automated documentation quality-control system cut false vital-sign entries from 9% to 1.33%, incomplete medication records from 3.33% to 1.67%, and missed assessment entries from 8% to 1.33%, while also cutting review time per record from over four minutes to about a minute. SafeFlow's documentation cue draws on this as evidence that structured, technology-supported checks can meaningfully reduce the everyday documentation gaps that make later reviews harder."
  },
